<<<
vowel & not farther; greet & deceit, shepherd & leopard, quality & frivolity, stationery & probationary, are rhymes; seat & deceit, station & crustacean, visible & invisible, are not.
===
vowel & not farther; *greet* & *deceit*, *shepherd* & *leopard*, *quality* & *frivolity*, *stationery* & *probationary*, are rhymes; *seat* & *deceit*, *station* & *crustacean*, *visible* & *invisible*, are not.
>>>

<<<
as phase & race, love & move & cove, are often treated as rhyming, but are called imperfect rhymes. Onesyllable rhymes are called male or masculine or single, two-syllable female or feminine or double, threesyllable & four-syllable triple & quadruple.
===
as *phase* & *race*, *love* & *move* & *cove*, are often treated as rhyming, but are called imperfect rhymes. One-syllable rhymes are called *male* or *masculine* or *single*, two-syllable *female* or *feminine* or *double*, three-syllable & four-syllable *triple* & *quadruple*.
>>>

<<<
rhyme royal (Pros.). A metre in stanzas of seven five-foot iambic lines rhyming ababbec. Chaucer’s Clerk’s Tale is a well-known example:
===
*rhyme royal* (Pros.). A metre in stanzas of seven five-foot iambic lines rhyming ababbcc. Chaucer's *Clerk's Tale* is a well-known example:
